sult=(0, out, ''))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, [])\n    assert result == {}\n    assert len(module.warnings) == 1\n```\n\nHmm, prefixes=[] \u2014 cmd = ['/sbin/sysctl']. Fine.\n\nWait, but actually there's a subtle bug-ish behavior: after a failed split on the first line, key='' and value=''. Then if a later line is multiline (starts with space), value += '\\n' + line -> value = '\\n\\t...'. Not needed.\n\nAlso `test_get_sysctl_run_command_raises`:\n\n```python\ndef test_get_sysctl_run_command_raises_ioerror():\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_exc=IOError('sysctl exploded'))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n    assert result == {}\n    assert module.warnings == ['Unable to read sysctl: sysctl exploded']\n```\n\nAnd OSError variant \u2014 since the except clause catches (IOError, OSError), and in Python 3 IOError is an alias of OSError, testing one covers it. But to be thorough, maybe parametrize over [IOError('x'), OSError('y')]. Since IOError IS OSError in py3, one test suffices. I could parametrize anyway for clarity. Let me parametrize:\n\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize('exc', [IOError('boom'), OSError('boom')])\ndef test_get_sysctl_run_command_raises(exc):\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, IOError('boom') is an OSError instance. Fine.\n\nAnd nonzero rc:\n\n```python\ndef test_get_sysctl_nonzero_rc_returns_empty():\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(1, 'kernel.a = 1\\n', 'some error'))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n    assert result == {}\n    assert module.warnings == []\n```\n\nAnd empty output:\n\n```python\ndef test_get_sysctl_empty_output():\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(0, '', ''))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n    assert result == {}\n    assert module.warnings == []\n```\n\nAlso maybe a test where get_bin_path returns a different path to verify cmd construction... covered by test 1's assertion.\n\nAlso, should I test that `prefixes` with multiple entries extends correctly? test 1 uses ['-a']; maybe use ['-n', 'kernel.hostname']? Eh, one is fine. Actually let me use prefixes=['-a'] in most tests and assert the cmd in test 1.\n\nNow, about the import: the snippet shows the file has `import re` and `from ansible.module_utils._text import to_text` then `def get_sysctl(...)`. The module path is `ansible.module_utils.facts.sysctl`. I'll import `from ansible.module_utils.facts.sysctl import get_sysctl`.\n\nOne concern: importing ansible.module_utils.facts.sysctl might trigger imports of other things? The file only imports re and to_text. `ansible.module_utils._text` imports... it might import `ansible.module_utils.six` or similar. Should be fine in an environment with ansible installed.\n\nLet me also double-check `to_text` behavior with an exception: `to_text(e)` where e is OSError('sysctl exploded'). to_text implementation:\n\n```python\ndef to_text(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogate_or_strict'):\n    if isinstance(obj, bytes):\n        ...\n    elif isinstance(obj, text_type):\n        return obj\n    elif isinstance(obj, Exception):\n        try:\n            return to_text(str(obj), ...)\n        except ...\n    else:\n        return text_type(obj)\n```\n\nHmm, actually in newer ansible, to_text handles Exception specially? Let me recall ansible.module_utils._text.to_text:\n\n```python\ndef to_text(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogate_or_strict'):\n    \"\"\"Make sure string is native text type...\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(obj, bytes):\n        return obj.decode(encoding, errors)\n    elif isinstance(obj, text_type):\n        return obj\n    else:\n        return text_type(obj)\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember an Exception branch. Actually there IS special handling in some versions:\n\nLooking at ansible/module_utils/_text.py (ansible-core):\n\n```python\nimport codecs\n\ndef to_bytes(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogate_or_strict'):\n    ...\n\ndef to_text(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogate_or_strict'):\n    if isinstance(obj, bytes):\n        return obj.decode(encoding, errors)\n    elif isinstance(obj, text_type):\n        return obj\n    elif isinstance(obj, Exception):\n        try:\n            return to_text(str(obj), encoding, errors)\n        except (UnicodeDecodeError, UnicodeEncodeError):\n            ...\n    else:\n        # new in python3, don't use str() on objects\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I recall:\n\n```python\ndef to_text(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogate_or_strict'):\n    \"\"\"Make sure string is text type\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(obj, bytes):\n        return obj.decode(encoding, errors)\n    elif isinstance(obj, text_type):\n        return obj\n    elif isinstance(obj, Exception):\n        try:\n            return to_text(str(obj), encoding, errors)\n        except Exception:\n            return to_text(repr(obj), encoding, errors)\n    else:\n        return text_type(obj)\n```\n\nSomething like that. Either way, str(OSError('sysctl exploded')) == 'sysctl exploded', so to_text gives 'sysctl exploded'. Good \u2014 my assertion `module.warnings == ['Unable to read sysctl: sysctl exploded']` should hold. To be safe, maybe assert `'Unable to read sysctl:' in module.warnings[0]` and `'sysctl exploded' in module.warnings[0]`. That's more robust. I'll do the safer substring assertions.\n\nSimilarly for the split-failure warning: assert startswith('Unable to split sysctl line') and the line in it.\n\nNow, the multiline test: out = 'kern.clockrate: hz 1000\\n\\ttick 1000\\n'. splitlines -> ['kern.clockrate: hz 1000', '\\ttick 1000']. \n\n- line 1: not blank; doesn't start with ' '; key='' -> skip; split on ': ' -> ['kern.clockrate', 'hz 1000'] -> key='kern.clockrate', value='hz 1000'\n- line 2 '\\ttick 1000': not blank (strip -> 'tick 1000'); startswith(' ') True -> value = 'hz 1000' + '\\n' + '\\ttick 1000' = 'hz 1000\\n\\ttick 1000'; continue\n- end: key truthy -> sysctl['kern.clockrate'] = 'hz 1000\\n\\ttick 1000'.strip() = 'hz 1000\\n\\ttick 1000'\n\nAssert result == {'kern.clockrate': 'hz 1000\\n\\ttick 1000'}. \n\nAlso a multiline case where the multiline continuation is followed by another key, to exercise line 51-52 after a multiline:\n\nout = 'kernel.a = 1\\n\\tcont\\nkernel.b = 2\\n':\n- 'kernel.a = 1': key='' -> split -> key='kernel.a', value='1'\n- '\\tcont': multiline -> value='1\\n\\tcont'\n- 'kernel.b = 2': key truthy -> sysctl['kernel.a'] = '1\\n\\tcont'; split -> key='kernel.b', value='2'\n- end: sysctl['kernel.b']='2'\n\nResult {'kernel.a': '1\\n\\tcont', 'kernel.b': '2'}. This covers 51-52 in combination with multiline. I could merge this into the multiline test. Let me make the multiline test use this richer output to cover more branches in one test:\n\n```python\ndef test_get_sysctl_multiline_value():\n    out = 'kernel.a = 1\\n\\tcontinued\\nkernel.b = 2\\n'\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(0, out, ''))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n    assert result == {'kernel.a': '1\\n\\tcontinued', 'kernel.b': '2'}\n```\n\nWait: value after multiline = '1' + '\\n' + '\\tcontinued' = '1\\n\\tcontinued'. strip() -> '1\\n\\tcontinued' (no leading/trailing ws). Good.\n\nHmm, but I also want the ': ' separator variant covered \u2014 the regex alternation `: `. In test with 'kern.clockrate: hz 1000'. Let me include a line using ': ' in the basic test:\n\nout = 'kernel.hostname = host1\\nkern.clockrate: hz 1000\\n' -> {'kernel.hostname': 'host1', 'kern.clockrate': 'hz 1000'}. That covers both separator forms. \n\nAlso the `\\s?=\\s?` with no spaces: 'a=b' -> ['a','b']. Could add 'kernel.version=4.1' line. Let me add to basic test: out = 'kernel.hostname = host1\\nkern.clockrate: hz 1000\\nvm.swappiness=10\\n' -> three keys. Good coverage of regex variants.\n\nNow let me also think: do we need a test where `line.strip()` is falsy but line is non-empty, e.g., '   ' (whitespace only)? `not line.strip()` -> True -> continue. My blank-lines test uses '\\n   \\n...' which includes '   '. Good \u2014 covers both truly empty and whitespace-only.\n\nWait, splitlines on '\\n   \\nkernel.hostname = host1\\n\\n' -> ['', '   ', 'kernel.hostname = host1', '']. Good.\n\nNow the FakeModule.get_bin_path assertion `assert name == 'sysctl'` \u2014 that's fine inside the fake.\n\nShould I also verify that run_command is called exactly once? run_command_calls list length check. In test 1: `assert module.run_command_calls == [['/sbin/sysctl', '-a']]`. Good.\n\nNow, about \"clean up after the test\" \u2014 nothing global is touched. The FakeModule instances are local. No files created. No monkeypatching needed. I might add a comment noting that. The instructions say to make sure to clean up to avoid state pollution; since we don't patch anything, we're fine. But maybe to demonstrate good practice, I could avoid module-level mutable state. The FakeModule class is stateless at module level. Good.\n\nAlternatively, use `unittest.mock.MagicMock` \u2014 but plain fake is clearer for assertions on warnings.\n\nLet me also consider: should tests be in a class or plain functions? Plain functions with pytest style.\n\nOne more consideration \u2014 the instruction says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\". Let me enumerate branch coverage per test:\n\nTest A (success, multiple separators):\n- 25,26,27,29,31,32,37(T),38,39,40,41(F),44(F),51(F first, T later),52,54,55,59(T),60,62 \u2713\n\nTest B (blank lines):\n- 41(T),42 \u2713 (plus A's lines)\n\nTest C (multiline):\n- 44(T),48,49 \u2713; also 51(T),52 after multiline \u2713\n\nTest D (unsplittable):\n- 56,57 \u2713; 51(T),52 \u2713\n\nTest E (unsplittable first line only):\n- 59(F) with non-empty output... wait, in test E, out='garbage...' -> loop runs, split fails, key stays '' -> line 59 F. Also covers 56,57. And result {}.\n\nTest F (empty output):\n- loop zero iterations, 59(F) \u2713\n\nTest G (exception):\n- 33,34,35,37(F),62 \u2713\n\nTest H (nonzero rc):\n- 37(F) \u2713\n\nThat covers everything. Lines 25-62 all executed across tests. \n\nNow, could I reduce the number of tests? The instruction says create test functions that execute all missing lines and branches \u2014 multiple tests are fine. I'll keep ~7 focused tests. Maybe merge E into D? D already covers 56,57 and 51/52. E covers the 59(F) with nonempty output. F covers 59(F) with empty output. Both are cheap; keep both? E's result {} assertion is a nice postcondition. Keep.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: in test D, after the failed split, key remains 'kernel.a' and value '1'. Then line 'kernel.b = 2' triggers 51-52 storing 'kernel.a' again. Fine.\n\nLet me also double check `re.split(r'\\s?=\\s?|: ', 'kernel.hostname = host1', maxsplit=1)`:\n- Pattern: `\\s?=\\s?` \u2014 matches ' =' (space, equals) at index 15... The string 'kernel.hostname = host1': positions: 'kernel.hostname' is 15 chars (k-e-r-n-e-l-.-h-o-s-t-n-a-m-e = 15), then ' = host1'. `\\s?=\\s?` greedily matches ' =' plus optional trailing space? `\\s?` after '=' matches the space after '='. So match is ' = ' (3 chars). split -> ['kernel.hostname', 'host1']. Good.\n\n'kern.clockrate: hz 1000': alternation tries `\\s?=\\s?` first \u2014 no '=' in string, fails; then ': ' matches at index 13. split -> ['kern.clockrate', 'hz 1000']. Good.\n\n'vm.swappiness=10': `\\s?=\\s?` matches '=' -> ['vm.swappiness', '10']. Good.\n\nNow the final code. Also, should I guard the import with pytest.importorskip('ansible')? If ansible isn't installed, tests error. The task assumes the ansible source is being tested, so plain import is fine. Maybe add `pytest.importorskip` for robustness? Hmm, it could mask real import errors. I'll do a plain import.\n\nLet me write the final script:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for ansible.module_utils.facts.sysctl.get_sysctl covering lines 25-62.\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.facts.sysctl import get_sysctl\n\n\nclass FakeModule:\n    \"\"\"Minimal fake AnsibleModule: records calls, no global state touched.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, run_command_result=None, run_command_exc=None,\n                 bin_path='/sbin/sysctl'):\n        self._run_command_result = run_command_result\n        self._run_command_exc = run_command_exc\n        self._bin_path = bin_path\n        self.warnings = []\n        self.run_command_calls = []\n\n    def get_bin_path(self, name):\n        assert name == 'sysctl'\n        return self._bin_path\n\n    def run_command(self, cmd):\n        self.run_command_calls.append(list(cmd))\n        if self._run_command_exc is not None:\n            raise self._run_command_exc\n        return self._run_command_result\n\n    def warn(self, message):\n        self.warnings.append(message)\n\n\ndef test_get_sysctl_success_parses_all_separator_styles():\n    out = ('kernel.hostname = host1\\n'\n           'kern.clockrate: hz 1000\\n'\n           'vm.swappiness=10\\n')\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(0, out, ''))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n\n    assert result == {\n        'kernel.hostname': 'host1',\n        'kern.clockrate': 'hz 1000',\n        'vm.swappiness': '10',\n    }\n    # cmd must be [sysctl binary] + prefixes\n    assert module.run_command_calls == [['/sbin/sysctl', '-a']]\n    assert module.warnings == []\n\n\ndef test_get_sysctl_skips_blank_lines():\n    out = '\\n   \\nkernel.hostname = host1\\n\\n'\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(0, out, ''))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n\n    assert result == {'kernel.hostname': 'host1'}\n    assert module.warnings == []\n\n\ndef test_get_sysctl_multiline_values_are_joined_with_newline():\n    out = 'kernel.a = 1\\n\\tcontinued line\\nkernel.b = 2\\n'\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(0, out, ''))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n\n    assert result == {'kernel.a': '1\\n\\tcontinued line', 'kernel.b': '2'}\n    assert module.warnings == []\n\n\ndef test_get_sysctl_unsplittable_line_warns_and_keeps_going():\n    out = 'kernel.a = 1\\ngarbage line without separator\\nkernel.b = 2\\n'\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(0, out, ''))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n\n    # previous pair is flushed before the bad line; later lines still parse\n    assert result == {'kernel.a': '1', 'kernel.b': '2'}\n    assert len(module.warnings) == 1\n    assert module.warnings[0].startswith('Unable to split sysctl line')\n    assert 'garbage line without separator' in module.warnings[0]\n\n\ndef test_get_sysctl_unsplittable_first_line_yields_empty_dict():\n    out = 'garbage line without separator\\n'\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(0, out, ''))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, [])\n\n    assert result == {}\n    assert len(module.warnings) == 1\n    assert module.warnings[0].startswith('Unable to split sysctl line')\n\n\ndef test_get_sysctl_empty_output_yields_empty_dict():\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(0, '', ''))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n\n    assert result == {}\n    assert module.warnings == []\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize('exc', [IOError('sysctl exploded'), OSError('sysctl exploded')])\ndef test_get_sysctl_run_command_exception_warns_and_returns_empty(exc):\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_exc=exc)\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n\n    assert result == {}\n    assert len(module.warnings) == 1\n    assert module.warnings[0].startswith('Unable to read sysctl:')\n    assert 'sysctl exploded' in module.warnings[0]\n    # run_command was still attempted with the assembled command\n    assert module.run_command_calls == [['/sbin/sysctl', '-a']]\n\n\ndef test_get_sysctl_nonzero_rc_returns_empty_dict():\n    module = FakeModule(run_command_result=(1, 'kernel.a = 1\\n', 'boom'))\n    result = get_sysctl(module, ['-a'])\n\n    assert result == {}\n    assert module.warnings == []\n```\n\nWait \u2014 in the exception test, run_command_calls: the fake appends the cmd before raising. Yes, `self.run_command_calls.append(list(cmd))` happens before the