ntry is multiline (flush happens at line 59-60 after loop):\n\nout = 'kernel.a = 1\\n continued\\n' \u2192\n- 'kernel.a = 1': key='kernel.a', value='1'\n- ' continued': multiline \u2192 value='1\\n continued'\n- end: sysctl['kernel.a'] = '1\\n continued'\n\nI can merge this into the multiline test by having two multiline entries? Let me restructure the multiline test:\n\nout = 'kernel.a = 1\\n cont1\\nkernel.b = 2\\n cont2\\n'\n- 'kernel.a = 1' \u2192 key='kernel.a', value='1'\n- ' cont1' \u2192 value='1\\n cont1'\n- 'kernel.b = 2' \u2192 flush sysctl['kernel.a']='1\\n cont1'; key='kernel.b', value='2'\n- ' cont2' \u2192 value='2\\n cont2'\n- end flush: sysctl['kernel.b']='2\\n cont2'\n\nResult: {'kernel.a': '1\\n cont1', 'kernel.b': '2\\n cont2'}. This covers multiline flush both mid-loop (51-52) and at end (59-60). \n\nNow, the value.strip() behavior: '1\\n cont1'.strip() \u2192 '1\\n cont1'. Good.\n\nLet me also think about whether `value.strip()` could matter: if continuation lines have trailing spaces, e.g. ' cont1  ' \u2192 value='1\\n cont1  ' \u2192 strip \u2192 '1\\n cont1'. Could add but not necessary.\n\nNow finalize the test list:\n\n1. test_get_sysctl_success_parses_all_separator_styles \u2014 basic, cmd assertion, no warnings.\n2. test_get_sysctl_skips_blank_lines \u2014 blank/whitespace lines.\n3. test_get_sysctl_multiline_values_are_joined_with_newline \u2014 multiline mid and end.\n4. test_get_sysctl_unsplittable_line_warns_and_keeps_going \u2014 warn branch + flush before bad line.\n5. test_get_sysctl_unsplittable_first_line_yields_empty_dict \u2014 warn + nothing stored.\n6. test_get_sysctl_empty_output_yields_empty_dict \u2014 zero-iteration loop.\n7. test_get_sysctl_run_command_exception_warns_and_returns_empty \u2014 parametrized IOError/OSError.\n8. test_get_sysctl_nonzero_rc_returns_empty_dict \u2014 rc!=0.\n\nThat's comprehensive. All lines 25-62 covered, all branches.\n\nWait, one more: line 26-27 with empty prefixes \u2014 covered in test 