OError, OSError) as e:\n        module.warn('Unable to read sysctl: %s' % to_text(e))\n        rc = 1\n\n    if rc == 0:\n        key = ''\n        value = ''\n        for line in out.splitlines():\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n\n            if line.startswith(' '):\n                # handle multiline values, they will not have a starting key\n                # Add the newline back in so people can split on it to parse\n                # lines if they need to.\n                value += '\\n' + line\n                continue\n\n            if key:\n                sysctl[key] = value.strip()\n\n            try:\n                (key, value) = re.split(r'\\s?=\\s?|: ', line, maxsplit=1)\n            except Exception as e:\n                module.warn('Unable to split sysctl line (%s): %s' % (to_text(line), to_text(e)))\n\n        if key:\n            sysctl[key] = value.strip()\n\n    return sysctl\n```\n\nNow, I need to write tests. I should use a mock module object. Let me think about what the module needs:\n- `get_bin_path('sysctl')` -> returns a path string\n- `run_command(cmd)` -> returns (rc, out, err) or raises IOError/OSError\n- `warn(msg)` -> records warnings\n\nI can create a simple mock class or use unittest.mock.MagicMock.\n\nLet me check the get_info tool for the function to understand better. Actually, the instructions say to use get_info as necessary. Let me call get_info on 'get_sysctl' to see what info it returns.\n\nLet me plan the tests:\n\nTest 1: `test_get_sysctl_success` - normal path with rc=0, output with multiple lines:\n```\nkernel.hostname = myhost\nkernel.osrelease = 4.1\n```\nThis covers lines 25-27, 29, 31-32, 37-40, 41 (strip check false), 44 (startswith false), 51 (key empty first time), 54-55, 59-60, 62.\n\nWait, for the first line, key is '' so line 51 `if key:` is False. For the second line, key is 'kernel.hostname' so line 51-52 executes. Then at the end, line 59-60 executes with the last key.\n\nTest 